From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ios-blink-sideload <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:43:08 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS sideload: select text like Safari

RenderWidgetUIView already uses BrowserEngineKit's text interaction,
which draws the system selection handles and presents the system edit
menu, but neither worked: a long press selected a word with no handles
to adjust it, and patch 0015's menu offered only Copy and Select All.

The system text interaction keeps its own copy of the selection, built
from the UITextInput methods, and these lost it:
- BETextRange returned the selection's end as its start, and
  -comparePosition:toPosition: and -offsetFromPosition:toPosition: said
  that all positions are the same, so every selection was a caret.
- -beginningOfDocument returned nil. UIKit turns the selection into
  offsets from the start of the document and back, and lost it there.
- The copy is refreshed with -refreshKeyboardUI, which only ran when
  the selected text changed. Blink reports that before the selection's
  new bounds, so the copy kept the old bounds or none. Refresh it when
  the bounds change too; refreshing dismisses the edit menu, so show it
  again if it was showing.
- -selectionClipRect returned CGRectNull outside text fields, which
  clipped the selection UI away. Clip to the view.
The handles now show, and the edit menu sits next to the selection.

Dragging a handle moved the wrong end of the selection: `baseIsStart`
(the start stays put while the end handle moves) was read the other way
round, and the end that stays put was taken again from the changing
selection at every move, so it wandered once the handles crossed. Take
it once, when the drag starts, from the middle of its bound.

Make the edit menu Safari's: let the system present it
(automaticallyPresentEditMenu), show it again when a handle is let go
and when a scroll ends, and hide it while the page scrolls.
-canPerformAction:withSender: now answers for the current selection, and
only for actions this class implements (UIKit's categories make every
responder respond to some private ones). The menu also gets commands
that other responders in the chain can perform, so keep only these:
Copy, Look Up, Translate and Share, plus Cut, Paste, Select, Select All
and Learn in text fields. Look Up and Translate come with UIKit's
private actions; send them to the BETextInput ones (-lookup:,
-translate:), which present the system dictionary and Apple Translate,
and add -share: and -addShortcut:. Look Up also needs the selected text
from -requestDocumentContext:completionHandler:, which now returns the
selection and the text Blink reported around it.

Status: verified at Chromium 4558926c in the iOS 27 simulator. A long
press on a word selects it with handles at both ends and shows Copy,
Look Up and Translate, with Share on the menu's second page; dragging
either handle, also across lines, changes that end of the selection and
the menu comes back over the new selection when the handle is let go;
the menu hides while the page scrolls and comes back, with the handles
following the text; Copy puts the selection on the pasteboard, Look Up
shows the system dictionary sheet and Translate Apple Translate's. In a
textarea the menu has Cut, Copy, Paste and Select All. A long press on
a link still shows Chrome's link menu.

